  "query_preview": "..High pressure will remain across the region through \nthe first half of the week. Very warm temperatures will continue \nwith highs around 10-20 degrees above normal through Tuesday. Some \nslightly cooler air arrives for the second half of the week but \ntemperatures will still remain around 5-10 degrees above normal. \n\nBy Tuesday, the high starts to shift to the east with a shortwave \nmoving through the mid-level flow. This wave will track along the \nwestern periphery of the ridge before starting to push eastward on \nWednesday, eventually crossing the region. Winds turn to the south \nto southwest, allowing for better moisture advection. This will \nmostly result in increasing cloudiness starting on Tuesday but a few \nisolated showers or sprinkles may occur near the White Mountains and \nalong the Mogollon Rim. By Wednesday, moisture increases further and \nsome isolated showers or dry thunderstorms will become possible, \nmainly along and north of Grand Canyon-Flagstaff-Show Low line. \nAdditionally, the gradient will tighten up for Wednesday resulting \nin southwest winds 15-25 mph with gusts up to 40 mph possible. Even \nthough we do see moisture increase, the prevailing conditions are \ndry. In these areas, mainly across Yavapai and western Coconino \ncounties, we will see enhanced fire weather concerns with near-\ncritical conditions possible. \n\nThursday and beyond...behind the wave the flow looks to become more \nzonal and begins to weaken. Temperatures will remain cooler than \nthose at the start of the week but will remain above normal through \nthe weekend. Dry conditions will once again prevail with \ntypical afternoon breezes each day.",
